theory_blocks.body:
A real experiment does not hand you ion symbols; it hands you what you can see, smell or test for. For molten lead(II) bromide: the cathode product is a dense, shiny, silvery liquid bead (molten lead);

the anode product is a choking, orange-brown fume (bromine vapour) that bleaches damp litmus paper. Naming 'lead' and 'bromine' is the explanation;

the colour, state and smell are the observations that justify it. A good answer always offers both: the name of the product, and the observation that supports it.

