theory_blocks.body:
A **hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell** takes two gases as continuous inputs, hydrogen and oxygen, and combines them to generate electricity directly, with water as the only chemical product: $$\mathrm{2H_{2} + O_{2} \rightarrow 2H_{2}O}$$

Checking the equation: 4 hydrogen atoms and 2 oxygen atoms appear on each side, so it is balanced. Three roles to keep separate: **hydrogen and oxygen** are the inputs (the fuel and the oxidant, supplied continuously);

**electricity** is the useful output, drawn off to power whatever the cell is connected to; **water** is the only chemical substance produced, typically released as vapour or liquid.

Compare the fuel cell's equation with the electrolysis of water (this is what effectively happens when dilute sulfuric acid is electrolysed, met earlier in this unit): electrolysis is $\mathrm{2H_{2}O \rightarrow 2H_{2} + O_{2}}$, driven by electrical energy going in;

the fuel cell is $\mathrm{2H_{2} + O_{2} \rightarrow 2H_{2}O}$, with electrical energy coming out. The three substances involved, hydrogen, oxygen and water, are identical in both cases;

only the direction of the reaction and the direction of energy flow are reversed. This is exactly why a fuel cell is never itself a source of hydrogen: hydrogen is what electrolysis *produces* and what a fuel cell *consumes*,

the two opposite ends of the same pair of substances.

